on. Their groundbreaking discovery revolves around the concept of complementary angular blocking regions, a novel approach that has transformed the landscape of optical filtering. To grasp the significance of this innovation, let us first delve into the world of angular blocking regions. Imagine a filter as a gatekeeper, tasked with controlling the flow of light. Traditional filters rely on a single blocking region, which rejects light at a specific angle. However, this approach has its limitations, as it can lead to reduced transmission efficiency and increased losses. The genius of complementary angular blocking regions lies in its ability to overcome these limitations. By incorporating two or more blocking regions, each designed to reject light at complementary angles, the filter achieves unprecedented levels of precision and efficiency. This harmonious marriage of blocking regions enables the filter to selectively allow or reject light with unparalleled accuracy, opening up new avenues for applications in fields such as astronomy, biomedical imaging, and even national security. The significance of this innovation is underscored by the legal filing of the patent, a testament to the groundbreaking nature of this discovery. The filing of US patent 11366257 marks a pivotal moment in the history of optics, as it formally recognizes the pioneering work of the researchers involved.", "original_document_number": 1}, {"doc_id": "d2", "card_id": "d2:c0", "entity": "Optical filters complementary angular blocking regions", "scope": "significant event", "claim": "Optical filters complementary angular blocking regions were involved in the Commission of Justiciary's most baffling cases.", "answer": "Commission of Justiciary's most baffling cases", "quote": "The Commission of Justiciary, the centuries-old method of law enforcement employed in Scotland, had been shaken to its core by a series of baffling crimes. It was as if the perpetrators had vanished into thin air, leaving behind only whispers of their presence. Eilidh's thoughts were interrupted by a knock at the door. Her trusted colleague, Dr. Hamish MacPherson, a brilliant physicist with a passion for optics, entered the room. \"Eilidh, I think I've made a breakthrough,\" he said, his eyes sparkling with excitement. \"I've been analyzing the security footage from the latest heist, and I believe I've identified a pattern.\" Intrigued, Eilidh leaned forward as Hamish explained his theory. \"You see, the cameras captured a faint, angular distortion in the light around the time of the theft. It's as if the perpetrators were using some sort of optical filter to manipulate the light, creating a blind spot in the surveillance.\" Eilidh's eyes narrowed. \"An optical filter, you say? That sounds like the work of a mastermind.\" Hamish nodded. \"I've been studying the patent records, and I think I've found a connection. There's a patent for 'Optical filters complementary angular blocking regions' that was filed around the same time the crimes began. It's a highly advanced technology, capable of blocking specific angles of light to create a 'blind spot' in the visual spectrum.\" Eilidh's mind raced as she connected the dots. \"So, you're saying that the perpetrators are using this technology to evade detection?\" Hamish nodded. \"It's the only explanation. And if we can trace the source of this technology, we might just uncover the identity of the mastermind behind the Commission of Justiciary's most baffling cases.\"", "original_document_number": 2}], "initial_answers": ["the filing of patent US 11366257", "the Commission of Justiciary's most baffling cases"]}
